**Svelte Data Table Component Creation**

Now, let's create the Svelte component.  I'll assume the API endpoint is `/api/data`.

```svelte
<!-- src/lib/DataTable.svelte -->
<script lang="ts">
  import { onMount } from 'svelte';

  let data: any[] = [];
  let loading = true;
  let error = null;

  onMount(async () => {
    try {
      const response = await fetch('/api/data');
      if (!response.ok) {
        throw new Error(`HTTP error! Status: ${response.status}`);
      }
      data = await response.json();
    } catch (e) {
      error = e;
      console.error("Error fetching data:", e);
    } finally {
      loading = false;
    }
  });
</script>

{#if loading}
  <p>Loading...</p>
{:else}
  {#if error}
    <p>Error: {error.message}</p>
  {:else}
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          {#each Object.keys(data[0] || {}) as key}
            <th>{key}</th>
          {/each}
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {#each data as row}
          <tr>
            {#each Object.keys(row) as key}
              <td>{row[key]}</td>
            {/each}
          </tr>
        {/each}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  {/if}
{/if}
```

**Reasoning:**

*   The component uses `onMount` to fetch data from the API endpoint `/api/data`.
*   It handles loading and error states.
*   It dynamically generates table headers and rows based on the structure of the fetched data.  This assumes all objects in the data array have the same keys.
*   The `Object.keys(data[0] || {})` part handles the case where `data` is initially empty.

